{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"content":"","reasoning_content":"Let me analyze the situation. There's a green square candidate (task:B) at pixel [156,248] in frame-00011. Wait, but the task says find blue ball A first. The target_candidates only has task:B. But the tracked points (point-001..008) all at same pixel [156,248] which is the same as the green candidate. Interesting — the tracked points are at the B candidate location, and their geometry is relative_xy_m [1.7354, 0.7421] with range 1.8874, bearing 0.4041.\n\nWait, the tracked points relative_xy_m = [1.7354, 0.7421]? That's forward 1.7354, left 0.7421 in body frame. But camera_xyz for B is [-0.7465, 0.3128, 1.4235] in optical frame. Convert to body: optical x right, y down, z forward. Body x forward = optical z = 1.4235. Body y left = -optical x = 0.7465. Yes matches relative xy [1.7354? hmm 1.4235 body forward + camera offset 0.32? 1.4235+0.32=1.7435 ≈1.7354]. Yes so tracking points are on the green square B.\n\nBut wait, the pixel [156,248] — image 640x360, so center u=320. u=156 is left of center, meaning the object is to the left. But bearing 0.4041 rad positive... Hmm bearing positive means left? Actually body y left positive, so bearing = atan2(0.7421, 1.7354) = 0.404 rad. Yes left.\n\nHmm, but pixel u=156 (left of center) with optical x = -0.7465 means the object is to the left. OK consistent.\n\nNow, the task requires finding A (blue ball) first. No blue candidate present. Current observation doesn't have a blue candidate. So A is not visible. But the task says \"留意两个目标\" before leaving initial area. Only B candidate visible.\n\nThe last action was a turn that failed due to invalid plan envelope. So we need to create a valid plan first.\n\nMemory has no confirmed objects (objects: []). So A is not confirmed. We need to explore to find blue ball A. But we have a candidate for B.\n\nStrategy: We should create a plan for exploration to find A. Since A isn't visible, we need to scan/explore. The instruction: \"If no target is visible, use current geometry to choose a safe scan/exploration action or abort.\" And \"Empty memory alone does not forbid exploration: use a valid new exploration plan with preconditions=[] and conservative turn/motion parameters.\"\n\nBut wait, we do have B candidate visible. But we need A first. We must find A. The blue ball is not in current observation. Maybe it's occluded by cabinets. We need to explore safely.\n\nHowever, we should also confirm B eventually but only after A. Actually order: first A, then B. So we must find A first.\n\nLet me think about what plan to submit. The plan needs subgoals with preconditions and completion. Let me create an exploration plan to scan for the blue ball.\n\nBut careful: task requires stopping at 0.82–1.18 m from A center, facing it, then B. We can't complete without finding A.\n\nGiven current state, the reasonable next action: create a new plan for exploration (scan). Set subgoal \"scan_for_A\" with preconditions=[] and conservative turn parameters. Then execute a turn to scan.\n\nActually the tool requirement: \"Motion and finish require a valid plan/subgoal binding.\" So turn requires plan. Let me submit a new plan with subgoal for scanning, then do a turn.\n\nBut I must output exactly one tool per turn plus the JSON. Actually \"Use exactly one supplied tool per turn with its existing argument schema. Also output a JSON object as assistant content.\" So I output text (the JSON plan) and call one tool? Hmm. \"Also output a JSON object as assistant content\". Let me re-read.\n\n\"You are the task-level planning agent... Use exactly one supplied tool per turn... Also output a JSON object as assistant content (no Markdown): {...}\"\n\nSo each turn: output JSON content AND call one tool? Or the JSON content is the plan and the tool call... Actually the JSON object is the plan/binding. The tool call is the action. Wait but it says \"Use exactly one supplied tool per turn.\" So we combine: the assistant message content is the JSON plan, and we make one tool call.\n\nHmm, but if we submit a new plan, the feedback assigns plan_id. So this turn we submit new plan (as JSON content) and call... but the plan submission itself needs a tool? Actually plan is submitted via the message content, not a tool. Then one tool call.\n\nLet me think. The system says: \"A new plan receives a harness-assigned plan_id in feedback. To continue it use {\"plan_id\":\"plan-0001\",\"subgoal_id\":\"inspect\"} with one tool call.\"\n\nSo the JSON as content encodes the plan. On a new plan, we just output the plan JSON, and then presumably the tool call is... Actually maybe the format is: output plan JSON as content, and call one tool that executes. But which tool? For a new exploration plan, we might call turn.\n\nBut the failed turn said \"Invalid plan envelope or binding. Observe or submit a valid new plan.\" So we need a valid plan first. I think this turn we should output a new plan JSON and probably call observe? Hmm.\n\nActually let me reconsider: maybe the tool call is the action and the JSON is the plan envelope attached. Let me just do: output the plan JSON as content, and call one tool. But if I call turn right after submitting new plan, is the plan active in same turn? Uncertain. Safer: output new plan + call observe to gather fresh evidence and get plan_id. But observe doesn't move. That's fine — observe is allowed without plan. And it would validate the plan envelope.\n\nActually, better approach: The instruction: \"When memory has no objects, do not wait by repeatedly calling observe. If an untracked target_candidate exists, call track_point at its current image_id and pixel, then inspect get_geometry/current tracking and obtain an independent frame to confirm it.\"\n\nWe have an untracked target_candidate for B at frame-00011, pixel [156,248]. But actually the tracked points are already at that pixel. Hmm, but the candidate requires \"track_point_then_independent_frame\". However B is not our first goal; A is.\n\nBut we can't find A. We should explore. But we might want to confirm B's location for later. Actually the task is ordered: A first. But leaving initial area risk. Let me focus: find A.\n\nSince no blue candidate visible, we need to rotate/scan. Let me create a scan plan and turn.\n\nLet me think about the plan structure. Subgoal: scan to find A. Completion conditions: can't verify from measurements easily. Completion is verified by memory from measurements. Hmm, we don't have A. So maybe subgoal \"find_A\" with completion... no object yet.\n\nMaybe a simpler plan: subgoal \"scan\" with preconditions=[] and completion tied to something. But completion must be verified by memory from measurements. Without object, we can't. Perhaps use pose.x etc.? Actually completion could be a pose measurement condition, but that's weird.\n\nLet me just make a plan focused on scanning toward the direction where the blue ball might be. Actually we don't know.\n\nLet me reason about geometry. The green square B is at body frame (1.735 forward, 0.742 left), ~1.89 m away. There may be cabinets nearby. The blue ball might be elsewhere, left or right.\n\nGiven the task said \"留意两个目标\" before leaving initial area — implying both might be visible initially. But only B candidate detected. Maybe the blue ball is present in image but not flagged as candidate? The target_candidates only flags B. Hmm.\n\nLet me actually just observe fresh — wait, we can observe to get new frame. But \"do not wait by repeatedly calling observe.\" One observe is fine to get current fresh evidence.\n\nActually current_observation is frame-00011 fresh. Let me instead create a scan plan and rotate to look around for blue ball.\n\nI think the cleanest first move: submit a new exploration plan (turn-based scanning), with a subgoal \"scan_left\" or general. Then call turn.\n\nBut I need a single tool call per turn and the plan JSON as content. Let me decide: This turn output plan JSON (new plan), and call turn to rotate? But turn might fail if plan binding not active yet. The failure earlier was because turn without valid plan. If I submit plan in same message, will binding work? The system: \"A new plan receives a harness-assigned plan_id in feedback.\" Feedback comes after the turn. So the plan isn't active until next turn's feedback? Then calling turn in same turn as new plan might fail again.\n\nSafer: This turn, submit new plan JSON and call observe (observe allowed without plan). Observe returns feedback including plan_id. Then next turn, use plan_id + turn.\n\nWait but does submitting a new plan happen via content only, and then observe validates it? The failed feedback says \"Observe or submit a valid new plan.\" So observe is a valid recovery. Let me do: output plan JSON, call observe. That both gives fresh observation and registers plan → feedback with plan_id.\n\nHmm, but actually observe may not register plan. Let me re-read: \"A new plan receives a harness-assigned plan_id in feedback. To continue it use {\"plan_id\":\"plan-0001\",\"subgoal_id\":\"inspect\"} with one tool call.\"\n\nSo when you submit a new plan (content JSON with \"plan\"), the harness in the next feedback assigns plan_id. The tool you call in that same turn—if observe—will just observe, and feedback includes plan_id. That works.\n\nActually maybe cleaner to just submit plan and call observe. Let me design the plan.\n\nPlan: exploration to locate blue ball A.\nSubgoals:\n- \"scan\": description \"rotate in place to scan for blue ball A\", preconditions [], completion... hmm need completion. Completion conditions must reference measurements or objects. Without A, we can't. Maybe completion can be a pose yaw threshold to bound the scan. E.g., complete after turning a certain amount. Use {\"measurement\":\"pose.yaw_rad\",\"op\":\"ge\",\"value\":...}. But that's a mechanism to limit.\n\nActually maybe I'm overcomplicating. The plan structure in the example:\n{\"plan\":{\"subgoals\":[{\"subgoal_id\":\"inspect\",\"description\":\"inspect a landmark\",\"preconditions\":[],\"completion\":[{\"object_id\":\"point-001\"}]}],\"branches\":[{\"on\":\"skill_stuck\",\"subgoal_id\":\"inspect\"}],\"recovery\":\"observe again\"},\"subgoal_id\":\"inspect\"}\n\nSo completion can be [{\"object_id\":\"point-001\"}]. It's verified by memory. Hmm.\n\nFor scanning, maybe I define subgoal \"scan_for_A\" with completion based on pose yaw. Let me use pose measurements which exist: pose.yaw_rad, pose.x_m, pose.y_m.\n\nGiven uncertainty, let me make a plan with conservative motions.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole situation. Maybe the intended approach: We DO have B candidate visible. The task wants A then B. But maybe A (blue ball) is actually what's being tracked? No, tracking points are on B (green).\n\nWait—re-examine. The target_candidates lists only task:B. But maybe the blue ball A is elsewhere and simply not yet detected because it's occluded. We need to scan.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the blue ball is the thing at a different location visible in the image but not flagged. Without color info I can't tell.\n\nLet me just do exploration: turn to scan for the blue ball. Since the initial observation seems to look somewhat forward-left at B, maybe rotate right to find A, or rotate left.\n\nI'll do a turn of +0.5 rad (left) or -0.5 (right)? Let me think: B is at bearing +0.404 (left). We want to find A; if A is not in current view, scanning right (negative yaw) might sweep the right side, or left. Hard to know.\n\nBetter: create a scan plan that does multiple turns as branches/recovery. But each turn is a separate tool call.\n\nLet me start with planning: This turn submit plan + observe to get plan_id and fresh frame. Actually current obs is already fresh (frame-00011, snapshot_lag 0). An additional observe might give frame-00012 with slightly different... but we haven't moved, so same view. That wastes. But it registers plan? Hmm.\n\nAlternative: Submit plan and call turn directly. If the harness binds the plan to the turn in the same message, it works. The earlier failure was because there was NO plan at all (active_plan_id: null). Now if I supply a plan in content, maybe it binds. But risk of another failure.\n\nGiven the explicit note \"A new plan receives a harness-assigned plan_id in feedback,\" I think plan submission is separate from action. So I should output the plan this turn, and the tool call this turn could be observe (allowed without plan) to gather fresh evidence while the plan registers.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the harness expects: assistant content = plan JSON OR binding JSON, and simultaneously one tool call. On the turn you submit a new plan, the tool call should be something allowed without plan (observe, track_point, get_geometry, stop, abort). On subsequent turns, use binding {\"plan_id\":..., \"subgoal_id\":...} + motion tool.\n\nSo plan: submit new plan, call observe. Then get plan_id. Then use binding + turn for scan.\n\nBut to save turns, maybe I can do get_geometry instead of observe (fresh via current_observation already). get_geometry returns tracked points. Tracked points are on B. Not helpful for A.\n\nLet me just submit plan + observe (one observation to confirm nothing changed / get a fresh frame). Actually let me instead call get_geometry? No, observe gives a new RGB frame possibly revealing blue ball that wasn't in candidate list. Hmm, but same view.\n\nActually, I realize the better first real action might be to turn to scan. But binding issue. Let me just do the safe thing: this turn = plan + observe. Wait, \"do not wait by repeatedly calling observe\" — one observe is fine.\n\nHmm, but is it wasteful? Let me instead combine: submit plan and call turn, trusting that plan binds. If it fails, I recover. Risk: another MEMORY_CONSTRAINT. But the instruction strongly implies new plan needs to be submitted first and gets plan_id in feedback before use.\n\nDecision: This turn, output new plan JSON as content, and call get_geometry? No. Let me call observe to get fresh frame (independent-ish) and register plan. Actually I'll call observe.\n\nWait, let me reconsider whether I even need a new plan to just observe. Observe can be called without plan (\"Observe/track_point/get_geometry/stop/abort may be used without a plan to gather evidence or recover\"). So I could observe now and separately plan later. But I must output a plan JSON as content each turn? \"Also output a JSON object as assistant content.\" For a no-plan action, maybe the content is just an observe intent? The example JSON includes \"plan\" and \"subgoal_id\". \n\nI think the cleanest: This turn output a valid new plan AND make one tool call. I'll make the tool call be observe to get a fresh frame and let plan register with feedback plan_id.\n\nHmm, but then the plan's subgoal must be executable soon. Let me define plan subgoals:\n\nSubgoal \"find_A\": description \"scan the area to locate blue ball A\", preconditions [], completion [{\"object_id\":\"task:A\"}] — but task:A is not a confirmed object yet, so completion can't be satisfied. That's okay; completion is what we aim for. Actually completion checked by memory; object task:A must be confirmed as object. Until then not complete.\n\nBranches: on \"skill_stuck\" → subgoal find_A; recovery \"observe and re-plan\".\n\nHmm. Let me think what \"find_A\" motion is. It's a scan. But plan doesn't encode motion per se. The subgoal is just a label; the actual tool calls (turns) I choose. Completion for find_A reference task:A. But then when I do turns, memory might pick up A. But memory won't automatically confirm A unless I track_point a blue pixel and it gets confirmed via independent frame.\n\nOK here's a thought: maybe the blue ball A IS visible but simply wasn't proposed as a candidate (color detector only flagged green). I should look at the image but I only have pixel candidates, no actual image pixels/colors. I can't see the image directly. I only get tool outputs. So I can't visually identify blue.\n\nGiven constraints, exploration via turning is the way. But without visual feedback of colors, I rely on target_candidates to update after I move/turn (the public context refresh). When I turn, a new frame will be provided with new target_candidates — maybe a blue candidate appears. So turning to scan and reading new target_candidates is the mechanism.\n\nGreat, so plan: rotate to scan, check new frame's target_candidates for blue.\n\nLet me set up plan and then execute turns.\n\nThis turn: submit plan + call observe? Or submit plan + call turn? I worry about binding. Let me look again at last_tool_feedback: the failed turn at step 44 says \"Invalid plan envelope or binding. Observe or submit a valid new plan.\" That turn was `turn` with no plan. So the fix: submit valid new plan. If I submit plan in content and call turn same message — is that \"submit a valid new plan\" + action? Possibly the harness processes content plan first then action. Uncertain.\n\nTo be safe and make progress, I'll submit the plan as content this turn and call observe (guaranteed allowed). The feedback should include plan_id. Then next turn, binding + turn.\n\nActually wait — if I observe without moving, the frame might be identical, and target_candidates identical. Wasted turn. But it secures plan_id. Acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, I could submit plan as content and immediately call turn — many such harnesses treat the content plan as the plan for that step's tool call. Let me consider the phrase \"A new plan receives a harness-assigned plan_id in feedback.\" This strongly suggests the plan_id comes in feedback AFTER submitting. And \"To continue it use {plan_id} with one tool call\" means subsequent turns reference it. So the submitting turn's tool call is separate. Therefore submit + observe.\n\nHmm, but then why would submitting a plan co-occur with observe? Because the harness gives feedback (plan_id) regardless of tool. Yes.\n\nLet me just go with: content = new plan JSON, tool = observe.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. The plan JSON also contains a top-level \"subgoal_id\":\"find_A\" which is the subgoal this turn's plan is about. And the example includes branches and recovery. Let me craft a coherent plan.\n\nPlan JSON:\n{\n \"plan\": {\n   \"subgoals\": [\n     {\"subgoal_id\":\"find_A\",\"description\":\"scan for and confirm blue ball A\",\"preconditions\":[],\"completion\":[{\"object_id\":\"task:A\"}]},\n     {\"subgoal_id\":\"approach_A\",\"description\":\"navigate to standoff 1.0m from A facing it\",\"preconditions\":[{\"object_id\":\"task:A\"}],\"completion\":[{\"measurement\":\"standoff_A\",\"op\":\"le\",\"value\":1.18}]},\n     ...\n   ],\n   \"branches\":[{\"on\":\"skill_stuck\",\"subgoal_id\":\"find_A\"}],\n   \"recovery\":\"observe a fresh frame and re-plan a scan direction\"\n },\n \"subgoal_id\":\"find_A\"\n}\n\nBut \"standoff_A\" measurement doesn't exist. Completion must be from memory measurements named in world.measurements. Available: pose.x_m, pose.y_m, pose.yaw_rad, point-00X.range_m, point-00X.bearing_rad. No standoff measurement. So completion for approach subgoals can't be expressed unless we track a point on A and use its range/bearing? The tracking point range measurement is range to that point, not necessarily standoff to center. Hmm.\n\nThis is tricky. The completion verification: \"Completion is verified by memory from measurements, never from your text.\" And conditions are object_id or measurement. For approaching A, we'd track a point on A, get point-XXX.range_m ~ 1.0. But we don't know the point id in advance. And standoff to center vs surface differs.\n\nMaybe the plan just needs subgoals with object preconditions; completion can be [{\"object_id\":\"task:A\"}] meaning \"A is confirmed\"? That doesn't capture standoff. But maybe the actual scoring is separate (independent evaluation). The plan's completion isn't the scoring. So plan completion is just for the agent's own bookkeeping / harness plan-progress. The episode success is determined by \"independent metrics.\"\n\nActually re-reading: \"finish remains a declaration; independent metrics determine episode success.\" And \"B only counts if A's independent checkpoint passed.\" So there's an external scorer. Our plan just needs to be valid envelope.\n\nSo I should make a reasonable plan. The harness likely only checks that subgoal_id and preconditions are consistent with confirmed evidence. Preconditions requiring task:A (unconfirmed) would fail if I claim them now. So for current find_A subgoal, preconditions=[].\n\nLet me keep plan minimal and valid: one subgoal \"find_A\" preconditions [], completion [{\"object_id\":\"task:A\"}], branches on skill_stuck → find_A, recovery.\n\nThen this turn call observe.\n\nWait, but the top-level \"subgoal_id\":\"find_A\" binds this turn's tool to find_A. But find_A has completion object task:A. Since we call observe (no motion), fine.\n\nActually, I realize there might be a subtlety: the harness might reject if the subgoal's completion is an object that isn't tracked, but I think that's fine — it's a goal.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe I should just do the whole thing more pragmatically. Let me plan broader with multiple subgoals for later, but focus.\n\nLet me now actually think about what the FIRST real useful action is. I'm going to:\nTurn 1 (this): submit plan + observe (get plan_id).\nTurn 2: binding find_A + turn yaw to scan.\n\nActually, can I reduce risk: Some harnesses let you submit plan and action in one. But given explicit plan_id-in-feedback, I'll separate.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more. Actually many of these \"plan binding\" harnesses: when you output content with \"plan\", that IS the plan, and then the tool call proceeds under that plan immediately. The \"plan_id in feedback\" is for continuing in subsequent turns