ys \"留意两个目标\" before leaving initial area, implying both visible now. But only B candidate. Possibly A is blue ball and color detector didn't flag because... it should flag blue too. Maybe A is occluded by a cabinet (\"目标可能被柜体遮挡\").\n\nGiven B is forward-left at 1.89m, and cabinets are obstacles, maybe A is behind/around. A conservative scan: turn right ~0.5 rad to sweep the right side, stopping to observe new candidates. But turning changes view and could reveal A.\n\nActually, maybe better to turn left toward B's area? No, we know B there.\n\nLet me just do a modest right turn of -0.5 rad to scan the right region. Wait, total turn limit is 0.6 rad per turn. Use 0.5.\n\nHmm, but which side has A? Unknown. Let me think: The image center is forward. B at u=156 (left of center 320). So B is left. The right side of the image (u>320) contains unclassified visual features at (521,190),(525,21),(412,46),(410,106),(408,178). These are just corners. A blue ball might be among them but not color-flagged. \n\nWithout color info I can't know. Let me do a scan: first turn slightly to bring more of the scene into view, then observe new candidates.\n\nActually maybe the smartest: The green square B is close/first visible. But we need A first. If A isn't visible, we must search. But maybe A is actually visible and I should track a blue pixel. I don't have blue pixel coordinates. So I can't track A.\n\nGiven the environment, exploration is required. Let me start scanning to the right (negative yaw) because B occupies left; the complementary right half may hold A or a passage.\n\nWait, actually reconsider: maybe turn LEFT would reveal more to the left where B is, but B already visible. Right side unexplored. Let me turn right -0.5.\n\nBut also cabinets as obstacles: turning in place is safe (no translation, no collision). Good, first action turn in place is collision-safe.\n\nLet me do plan + turn(-0.5). Hmm, but I'm slightly worried binding. Let me hedge: do plan + turn. If fails, recover with observe and re-submit.\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider doing observe first to be safe, because a failed turn wastes a step and the task emphasizes not waiting. But a failed turn isn't fatal. I'll go with plan + turn since it's the intended flow and makes progress.\n\nWait—one more consideration. If I turn -0.5 rad right, and A is actually to the left (near B), I might lose sight of B and not find A. But turning is reversible.\n\nLet me reconsider the geometry to guess A's location. Actually, the task hint: \"B 只有在 A 的独立检查点已通过后才计分，直接先到 B 不算完成\" — B counts only after A checkpoint. This suggests A and B in same general area, and a naive agent might be tempted to go to B (visible) first. A might be occluded behind a cabinet, requiring going around.\n\nI think the reasonable plan: scan to find A. Let me do an initial turn to the right to check the right side, then if nothing, turn back left / explore.\n\nActually, maybe better: turn left toward B's side first? No.\n\nLet me just commit: turn -0.5 (right) to scan.\n\nHold on, let me reconsider whether the blue ball might already be within view but the candidate detector needs a track_point. The instruction: \"If an untracked target_candidate exists, call track_point at its current image_id and pixel.\" Only B candidate exists (untracked). But we already have tracked points at B's pixel. So B is effectively being tracked. We shouldn't waste confirming B before A? Actually confirming B now could be useful since B is visible and we'll need it, and it doesn't violate order as long as we don't FINISH with B before A's checkpoint. \"直接先到 B 不算完成\" means going to B first doesn't complete the ordering check, but observing/tracking B now is fine and even wise (\"留意两个目标\").\n\nBut the true blocker is A unknown. Let me scan.\n\nAlright, let me also reconsider: is it possible that the task expects me to realize A is occluded and I must move to reveal it. Turning in place won't reveal occluded objects behind cabinets. I'd need to translate to go around. But translation risks collision without clearance knowledge. Conservative: turn first, then decide.\n\nOK final decision for this turn: Output new plan JSON, call turn(-0.5) — wait, but I concluded plan+turn. Let me reconsider risk one final time.\n\nIf plan+turn is wrong and requires plan_id first, I get failure feedback and retry with observe. Minor cost. If plan+turn is right, I make progress. Given the example strongly ties a subgoal to a tool call and says \"to continue use plan_id... with one tool call\", I'm fairly confident plan+turn works on the initial submission. Let me go.\n\nActually, the safest reading that satisfies \"exactly one tool per turn\" and yields progress: I'll submit plan and call turn. Let's do -0.5.\n\nHmm, wait. But is -0.5 even helpful? Let me instead reconsider turning LEFT by +0.5 to look more at where B is and beyond — maybe A is just left of B (further left). The tracked B is at u=156, quite far left already. Turning left brings left content to center, potentially revealing A near B. That might be more fruitful because A and B are likely clustered (\"留意两个目标\" in same area). Turning left to encompass the left region where B sits might reveal A adjacent.\n\nBut if A were near B and visible, the detector might have flagged blue. Unless occluded.\n\nArgh, uncertainty. Let me pick turning left +0.5 because A likely near B and to the left. Actually B at bearing +0.4 left, near left edge of a 640 image (u=156, center 320, half-width camera FOV ~ maybe 60°? u=156 is 164 px left of center, /320*30°≈15°, so bearing ~ -? hmm). Actually bearing 0.404 rad = 23°. Fine.\n\nLet me look at unclassified corners: left side u~135-247 region and right side u~408-525. These are just features/cabinets.\n\nI'll turn left +0.5 to bring the far-left (where B is and possibly A) into central view, then read new candidates.\n\nWait, but B is the green square, already tracked. If A is to the LEFT of B, turning left reveals it. If to the right, turning right. Coin flip.\n\nLet me think about the completion geometry for B: B center at body (1.735, 0.742). We'll need to end at 1.0m from B facing it. That's later.\n\nI'll choose to turn left +0.5 first, since A and B are described together and B is on the left, suggesting the interesting objects cluster on the left. Actually, hmm, but then why is A not visible? Because occluded by a cabinet. Turning left might not help with occlusion.\n\nGiven I really can't know, let me just do a rightward turn to explore the opposite side (cleaner sweep), OR... \n\nLet me just decide: turn LEFT is riskier because B already there. The unexplored region is the RIGHT half of image (u 408-525 features). So turning RIGHT (-0.5) brings the right region into center, which is where I have NOT confirmed any object. A could be there. Let me turn right.\n\nWait, but the half of the world not in the camera FOV at all (behind us or far right) is truly unexplored. Turning right sweeps toward it.\n\nDecision: turn(-0.5). Wait, actually, hmm, thinking about it more: the camera looks forward (body x). B is forward-left. If I turn right, B moves further left/out of view, and I see the forward-right quadrant. Reasonable to explore new area. Go.\n\nBut actually, maybe I'm overanalyzing a scan direction. Let me just do a modest turn and gather evidence. I'll pick turn right -0.5.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider using observe in the first turn instead of turn, then committing the scan direction after seeing a fresh frame with maybe A revealed. But same view → no new info. So turn.\n\nFINAL: content = new plan JSON (subgoal find_A), tool = turn(yaw_rad=-0.5).\n\nLet me write the plan. I'll include branches and recovery. Keep subgoals: find_A (completion task:A), and later approach/standoff ones with preconditions on task:A and task:B. But preconditions must be confirmed current evidence — task:A not confirmed, so those subgoals can't be executed now, but listing them is fine